<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Stiklestad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1030">1030</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Stiklestad was fought on 29 July 1030 in Trøndelag, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, where King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a>, returning from exile to reclaim his throne, was killed by an army of Norwegian farmers and chieftains loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a>. Olaf was soon venerated as a saint, and his cult as Saint Olaf, Norway's eternal king, helped establish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and royal authority in the country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-stiklestad.jpg" alt="Battle of Stiklestad" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Nicolai Arbo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arbo-Olav_den_helliges_fall_i_slaget_p%C3%A5_Stiklestad_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> created a unified Norwegian state at the end of the 9th century, but the union came apart after his death, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarls</a> of Lade and Harald's descendants spent the next century in feuds over power. In 1015 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a> returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> trips and was elected king, and in June 1016 he won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nesjar">Battle of Nesjar</a> against the jarls of Lade. In 1028 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> allied himself with the Lades and Olaf went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. After the last Lade jarl, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakon-eiriksson">Haakon Ericsson</a>, drowned in 1029, Olaf prepared an expedition, supported by the Swedish king Anund Jacob, who gave him 400 men and guides.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>According to saga sources, Olaf crossed the mountains with 3,600 men into the valley of Verdalen, about 80 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a>, and at the farm of Stiklestad met an army led by Hárek of Tjøtta, Thorir Hund and Kálfr Árnason, who had once served him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>, writing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a> about 200 years later, gives Olaf's war cry as Forward, men of Christ, men of the cross, men of the king, and the farmers' as Forward, farmers. According to Snorri, Olaf received three wounds: in the knee, in the neck and, as he leaned against a large stone, a spear thrust into his belly.</p><p>Snorri names Thorir Hund and Kálfr Árnason among those who gave the lethal wounds. Dagr Hringsson fought on after the king's death, until Kálfr and Thorir forced him and the rest of Olaf's army to retreat. Olaf's half-brother Harald, fifteen years old and later King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a>, was wounded and hidden by locals. The king's body was buried secretly in the sandy banks of the Nidelva River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>According to Snorri, the grave was opened the year after the battle and the body was found incorrupt. It was moved to St. Clement's Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a>, and Bishop Grimketel began the beatification of Olaf on 3 August. The rule of Cnut's wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelfgifu-of-northampton">Ælfgifu of Northampton</a> and their son Svein proved harsh, and the church came under pressure. Olaf's status as a martyr grew, and the common people revolted against Danish rule. When Olaf's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-the-good">Magnus the Good</a> claimed the throne, Cnut had to yield. About a century after the battle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidaros-cathedral">Nidaros Cathedral</a> was built on the site of Olaf's first burial.</p><p>Historians treat Snorri's account with caution. The sizes of the armies are probably impossible to determine, and archaeology has found little at Stiklestad. Olaf became Rex Perpetuus Norvegiae, the eternal king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and his cult helped complete the conversion of the country. The Saint Olav Drama has been staged at Stiklestad every year since 1954.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Stiklestad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Driven out by Cnut the Great, Olaf Haraldsson returned and was killed at the <b>Battle of Stiklestad</b> in 1030, and his rapid veneration as Saint Olaf gave the monarchy a sacred patron and a lasting focus of loyalty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
